he rings, and nose jewels,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙYthe changeable suits of apparel, and the mantles, and the wimples, and the crisping pins,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ>the glasses, and the fine linen, and the hoods, and the vails.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses‘„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙåAnd it shall come to pass, that instead of sweet smell there shall be stink; and instead of a girdle a rent; and instead of well set hair baldness; and instead of a stomacher a girding of sackcloth; and burning instead of beauty.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ;Thy men shall fall by the sword, and thy mighty in the war.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙWAnd her gates shall lament and mourn; and she being desolate shall sit upon the ground.¦layout ªlast_verse